      "id": 1,
      "text": "Saratoga was named in the 1880s after the famed New York spa town, inspired by a local spring believed to have healing powers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Saratoga,_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in Saratoga in 1884, marking the town’s first official federal presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Saratoga,_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Gulf, Colorado and Santa Fe Railway reached Saratoga in 1904, linking the community to the wider Gulf Coast region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saratoga%2C_Texas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cotton drilled an oil test well in 1865, but mechanical failures forced its abandonment before the town’s oil boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/saratoga-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early 20th‑century brine disposal from oil operations created saltwater marshes that now shape Saratoga’s wetlands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
